margin. And it is crucial to

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:52.920
clarify here for you guys

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:54.600
listening that this 0.35

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:56.760
index is not because the work of a

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:59.320
highly trained ICU nurse in San Juan

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:00.840
is inherently worth a third of the

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:01.960
work of a nurse in Florida.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:02.760
No, not at all.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:04.840
It is a mathematical artifact of a

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:06.360
totally flawed formula.

00:25:06.360 --> 00:25:08.040
Hospitals can only afford to pay

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:09.800
their staff based on the revenue

00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:10.440
they generate right.

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:11.800
Right, basic economics.

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:13.240
For many of these hospitals, their

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:14.600
primary sorts of revenue is

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.200
Medicare reimbursements.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:17.800
But Medicare reimburses them

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:19.000
based on what they are currently

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:20.120
paying their staff.

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:21.640
It's the ultimate catch 22.

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:24.920
Low Medicare reimbursement forces

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:26.680
the hospital to pay low wages just

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:28.120
to keep the lights on and the doors

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:28.360
open.

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:29.080
Yeah.

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:30.280
Those low wages are then

00:25:30.280 --> 00:25:31.640
dutifully recorded on a cost

00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:33.000
report and sent to CMS.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.080
And then CMS plugs those low wages

00:25:35.080 --> 00:25:35.880
into the formula.

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:38.440
Which generates an abysmal 0.35

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:39.240
wage index.

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:41.160
Which then dictates that the

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:43.320
hospital must receive low

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:44.520
Medicare reimbursement.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:46.440
The spreadsheet literally measures

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:47.720
the poverty it created in the

00:25:47.720 --> 00:25:48.600
first place and then

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:49.880
institutionalizes it.

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:50.680
Recisely.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:52.280
It locks them into a perpetual

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:53.960
cycle of underfunding.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:55.320
Now for a brief period from

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:57.400
roughly 2020 to 2024,

00:25:57.400 --> 00:25:58.360
the federal government

00:25:58.360 --> 00:25:59.960
actually attempted to intervene.

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:01.080
What did they do?

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:02.600
Well, the Centers for Medicare

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:04.600
and Medicaid Services or CMS

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:05.960
recognized that this circularity

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:07.320
trap was destroying hospitals

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:08.840
nationwide, not just in Puerto

00:26:08.840 --> 00:26:09.720
Rico.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:10.760
So they implemented what they

00:26:10.760 --> 00:26:12.440
called a low wage index

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:13.640
hospital policy.

00:26:13.640 --> 00:26:14.760
Okay, so effects.

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:15.160
Kind of.

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:16.040
This was essentially an

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:17.640
administrative band aid

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:18.920
that artificially boosted

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:20.600
the wage index for the lowest

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:22.040
quartile of hospitals across the

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:22.600
country.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.120
So they just arbitrarily raised

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:25.240
the floor for the poorest

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:26.280
hospitals.

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:28.520
Yes, they used administrative

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:29.960
discretion to just inflate the

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:31.640
numbers from the bottom up.

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:33.720
For Puerto Rico, this temporary

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:35.800
patch lifted their effective

00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:37.480
operational wage index

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:39.720
from that natural 0.35

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:41.320
up to around 0.61.

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:42.760
That's a huge jump.

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:43.640
It nearly doubled their

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:44.520
protection overnight.

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:46.200
It was a massive influx of

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:47.880
desperately needed stabilization

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:48.680
funding.

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:50.120
But the problem with administrative

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:51.640
patches is that they can be ripped

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:52.200
off.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:52.760
Very true.

00:26:52.760 --> 00:26:54.280
And that is exactly what happened

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:55.880
in July of 2024.

00:26:56.520 --> 00:26:58.600
The DC Circuit Court of Appeals

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:00.840
issued a major ruling in a case

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:02.440
called Bridgeport Hospital v.

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:03.000
Becerra.

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:05.400
What exactly was the legal

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:06.200
argument there?

00:27:06.200 --> 00:27:07.560
Why did a court strike down a

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:09.320
policy that was specifically meant

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:10.680
to help poor hospitals?

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:11.720
It all comes back to the

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:13.800
tension between statutory text

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:15.080
and administrative action,

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:16.040
much like the Nantucket

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:17.720
situation we talked about earlier.

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:18.920
In the Bridgeport case,

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:20.520
a group of higher paid hospitals

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:22.040
basically sued the Secretary of

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:23.080
Health and Human Services.

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:24.360
Why were they mad?

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:26.120
They argued that CMS did not have

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:27.400
the statutory authority

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:29.160
to artificially boost the wages

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:30.360
of poor hospitals.

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:31.640
Remember, budget neutrality.

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:32.440
Oh, right.

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:33.640
Because to boost the bottom,

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:34.760
25 percent?

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:36.200
CMS had to cut the payments

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:37.720
for the top 75 percent to pay

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:38.280
for it.

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:38.840
Wow.

00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:40.520
So the rich hospitals sued to

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:42.600
protect their slice of the pie.

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:43.160
Exactly.

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:45.080
The higher paid hospitals sued

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:46.680
arguing the statute requires

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:48.040
the index to be based on

00:27:48.040 --> 00:27:50.040
actual relative hospital wage

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:50.840
levels,

00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:53.000
not artificially inflated ones.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:54.840
And the D.C. Circuit agreed

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:56.200
they ruled that CMS

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:58.040
overstepped its legal bounds.

00:27:58.040 --> 00:27:59.240
And so the policy was struck

00:27:59.240 --> 00:27:59.640
down?

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:01.240
The policy was struck down.

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:02.680
The Band-Aid was ripped off.

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:03.880
And immediately there was

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:04.760
widespread panic

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:06.360
in healthcare finance circles.

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:07.720
I remember reading the headlines

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:10.360
about a 15.5 percent overnight

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:11.160
cliff.

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:12.680
People thought hospital funding

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:13.960
in Puerto Rico was just going

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:15.480
to plummet instantly the day

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:16.280
after the ruling.

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:17.560
That was the prevailing fear,

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:18.600
absolutely.

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:20.120
But we really have to correct

00:28:20.120 --> 00:28:21.160
that misconception

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:22.440
because the reality is actually

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:23.800
much more insidious

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:25.080
than a sudden cliff.

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:25.880
Okay, explain that.

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:27.000
It is not a sudden cliff.

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:28.760
CMS regulations have a built-in

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:29.880
shock absorber.

00:28:29.880 --> 00:28:31.000
By rule,

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:32.440
no hospital's wage index can

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:33.880
fall by more than 5 percent

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:35.720
in a single year, year over over.

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:37.320
So instead of dropping straight

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:39.400
from 0.61 all the way down

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:41.560
to 0.35 in one day,

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:42.600
it sort of steps down

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:43.320
incrementally.

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:43.960
Correct.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:45.480
Puerto Rico is facing a capped

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:46.520
glide path.

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:48.360
The wage index drops exactly

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:49.720
5 percent every single year.

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:51.160
Okay, let's do the math on that.

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:52.440
When you run a 5 percent wage

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:54.200
index cut through that 62 percent

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:55.560
labor share formula we discussed

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:56.280
earlier,

00:28:56.280 --> 00:28:58.360
it translates to roughly a 2 percent

00:28:58.360 --> 00:28:59.560
payment cut to the hospital's

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:00.440
total revenue.

00:29:00.440 --> 00:29:02.040
And that happens every October

00:29:02.040 --> 00:29:03.720
when the new fiscal year begins.

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:04.120
Yes.

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:05.160
But because of the distance

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:07.560
between their boosted 0.61 index

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:10.200
and their natural 0.35 index

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:11.560
is so vast,

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:12.840
they aren't just taking this cut

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:13.400
once.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:14.120
No.

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:15.880
They will hit that 5 percent cap

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:17.240
year after year after year.

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:18.680
This step down process will

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:20.280
continue for about 8 consecutive

00:29:20.280 --> 00:29:21.720
years until they finally hit the

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:22.440
absolute bottom.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:24.200
Eight years of compounding 2 percent

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:24.760
cuts.

00:29:24.760 --> 00:29:25.960
If we connect this to the bigger

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:26.680
picture,

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:28.120
a slow 2 percent bleed

00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:29.720
is actually far more dangerous

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:30.600
systemically

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.680
than a sudden 15 percent cliff.

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:33.960
I think I see we're going with

00:29:33.960 --> 00:29:34.440
this,

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:36.440
but explain why a smaller cut is

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:37.160
worse.

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:38.360
Because a cliff

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:40.520
creates an immediate undeniable

00:29:40.520 --> 00:29:41.080
crisis.

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:41.560
Right.

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:43.480
If revenues drop 15 percent

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:44.280
overnight,

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:45.720
it makes the front page of every

00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:46.680
newspaper.

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:49.080
It causes hospital CEOs to march on

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:49.960
Washington.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:51.800
It forces politicians to draft

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:53.160
emergency relief bills.

00:29:53.160 --> 00:29:53.720
Right.

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:55.800
A cliff is acute trauma.

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.240
And systems respond to acute

00:29:57.240 --> 00:29:58.200
trauma,

00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:00.520
but a steady 2 percent bleed

00:30:00.520 --> 00:30:01.880
every single year.

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:03.400
It avoids the headlines.

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:04.680
It doesn't trigger emergency

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.040
congressional hearings.

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:07.720
It just compounds quietly in

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:08.360
the background.

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:10.280
The system just slowly suffocates.

00:30:10.280 --> 00:30:11.080
Exactly.

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:12.200
The hospital doesn't close

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:12.600
overnight.

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:14.040
Instead, they just delay upgrading

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:15.240
the MRI machine.

00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:16.760
They freeze hiring for specialty

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:17.480
nurses.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:19.160
They quietly close a few beds on

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:20.440
the fourth floor.

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:21.880
By the time the public realizes

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:23.080
the system is fundamentally

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:23.960
collapsing,

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:25.240
the structural damage is already

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:25.640
done.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:26.920
And the talent has left the

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:27.320
island.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:27.480
Yeah.

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:28.760
The doctors moved to Florida

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:29.640
or Texas,

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:31.400
and it is incredibly difficult

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:32.840
to rebuild that from scratch.

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:34.600
It's literally the frog in boiling

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:35.800
water scenario.

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:36.920
But just when you think the

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:38.200
situation for Puerto Rico can't

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:39.160
get any worse,

00:30:39.160 --> 00:30:40.840
there is a second blade to

00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:42.120
this financial guillotine.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:43.000
Yes, there is.

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:44.680
These wage index cuts don't just

00:30:44.680 --> 00:30:46.680
hit traditional fee-for-service

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:47.240
Medicare.

00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:49.000
They hit Medicare Advantage.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:50.280
And for Puerto Rico,

00:30:50.280 --> 00:30:52.360
that is where the real devastation

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:53.080
lies.

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:54.600
This is the critical asymmetry of

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:55.880
Puerto Rico's healthcare system

00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:57.160
compared to the rest of the nation.

00:30:57.160 --> 00:30:58.600
On the U.S. mainland,

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:01.160
about 55.5% of Medicare

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:03.080
beneficiaries choose to enroll

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:04.120
in Medicare Advantage,

00:31:04.120 --> 00:31:05.640
which are these private plans

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:07.000
subsidized by the government.

00:31:07.000 --> 00:31:08.200
And the rest use traditional

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:08.760
Medicare.

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:09.160
Right.

00:31:09.160 --> 00:31:10.120
But in Puerto Rico,

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:11.560
it's 96.2%.

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:12.920
96.2%.

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:14.840
The vast overwhelming majority of

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:16.840
seniors rely on these private MA

00:31:16.840 --> 00:31:17.240
plans.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:19.560
Traditional Medicare is just a

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:21.160
tiny fraction of the market there.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:23.160
So how does a cut to a hospital's

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:25.000
traditional Medicare wage index

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:26.280
actually affect the private

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:27.880
Medicare Advantage plans?

00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:29.240
Because they are mathematically

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:29.880
linked.

00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:31.320
Medicare Advantage benchmarks,

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:32.520
which is the maximum amount of

00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:34.120
money Medicare will pay a private

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:35.880
plan per enrollee in a specific

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:36.760
county,

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:38.920
are calculated using local fee-for-service

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:39.720
spending data.

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:40.200
Oh.

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:41.720
That data is then adjusted by

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:42.360
something called the

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.040
Average Geographic Adjustment,

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:45.320
or AGA.

00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:47.080
And the AGA looks at a five-year

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:48.040
rolling average

00:31:48.040 --> 00:31:49.720
of historical claims data.

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:50.360
Wait.

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:52.200
If the AGA looks at a five-year

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:53.640
historical average,

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:54.920
wouldn't that dilute the impact

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:57.160
of a 5% wage index cut?

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:57.880
Like,

00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.040
if I have one bad year of data

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:01.880
mixed with four good years,

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:03.720
the average should soften the blow,

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:04.040
right?

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:05.960
It would only be one-fifth of the impact.

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:07.880
That is the highly logical assumption

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:09.160
that many policymakers

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:10.200
initially made.

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:11.800
But I'm guessing it's wrong.

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:13.240
It is entirely incorrect.

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:15.320
Because of a specific

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:16.840
repricing mechanism

00:32:16.840 --> 00:32:18.120
hidden deep in the formula.

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:19.400
Repricing mechanism.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:19.720
Yes.

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:22.440
When CMS calculates the AGA,

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:23.720
they don't just average out the

00:32:23.720 --> 00:32:25.080
dollar amounts that were actually

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:26.280
paid in the past.

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:27.480
They take those five years of

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:28.760
historical claims

00:32:28.760 --> 00:32:30.440
and they reprice them as if they

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:31.960
were being paid under today's

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:33.320
current wage index rules.

00:32:33.320 --> 00:32:34.040
Oh, wow.

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:36.200
So they retroactively apply the cut

00:32:36.200 --> 00:32:37.560
to the historical data.

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:38.280
Exactly.

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:39.320
They apply the current year's

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:40.760
depressed wage index cut

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:42.680
to the entire five-year historical

00:32:42.680 --> 00:32:43.400
base period.

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:45.000
So the cut doesn't get watered down at all?

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:45.640
Not at all.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:46.840
It hits the Medicare Advantage

00:32:46.840 --> 00:32:48.200
benchmark essentially in full.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:49.160
That is brutal.

00:32:49.160 --> 00:32:50.840
It reaches the MA benchmarks

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:52.040
essentially in full.

00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:53.320
About one calendar year

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:55.160
after the cut hits the hospitals.

00:32:55.160 --> 00:32:56.440
This is the multiplier effect

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:57.240
of the tragedy.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:58.760
Break that multiplier effect down.

00:32:58.760 --> 00:32:59.320
Sure.

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:00.520
A wage index cut

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:02.280
to a traditional Medicare system

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:04.760
that serves maybe 27,000 people

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:05.960
in Puerto Rico

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:07.560
flows directly into a formula

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:08.840
that dictates the funding

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:10.760
for over 600,000 seniors

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:12.040
enrolled in Medicare Advantage.

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:13.000
Oh, wow.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:14.680
It is a massive compounding

00:33:14.680 --> 00:33:16.120
drain of resources

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:17.880
that affects nearly every single

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:19.160
elderly person on the island.

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:19.400
Okay.

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:20.920
So the picture is incredibly grim.

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:22.200
The island is bleeding out

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:23.560
2% a year,

00:33:23.560 --> 00:33:24.840
quietly and relentlessly

00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:26.600
with massive downstream effects

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:28.760
on hundreds of thousands of seniors.

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:29.880
Which brings us all the way

00:33:29.880 --> 00:33:30.920
back to our starting point.

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:31.400
Feeek.

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:32.360
Right, the new hospital.

00:33:32.360 --> 00:33:33.480
How does an unopened

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.320
$85 million facility

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:37.160
on an island of 8,000 people

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:38.840
stop a macroeconomic crisis

00:33:38.840 --> 00:33:40.680
error for 3.2 million people?

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:41.480
It all comes back

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:42.840
to the Nantucket precedent.

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:45.240
The 1997 Rural Floor Statute

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:47.000
and the literal unapologetic text

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:47.960
of the law.

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:49.960
Remember, VX is geographically rural.

00:33:49.960 --> 00:33:51.160
It sits completely outside

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:52.440
the San Juan Metropolitan

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:53.400
Statistical Area

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.080
or any other MSA.

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:57.880
It is rural by pure geography,

00:33:57.880 --> 00:33:58.920
just like Nantucket.

00:33:58.920 --> 00:33:59.960
Exactly.

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:01.720
The day the new Susana Centino

00:34:01.720 --> 00:34:03.320
facility is officially licensed

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:04.440
by the Puerto Rican Department

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:05.080
of Health

00:34:05.080 --> 00:34:06.200
and subsequently Medicare

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:07.720
certified as a hospital.

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:09.240
It has to be a specific type

00:34:09.240 --> 00:34:10.040
of hospital, right?

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:10.760
Yes.

00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:12.840
Specifically, an inpatient

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:14.280
prospective payment system

00:34:14.280 --> 00:34:17.160
hospital, an IPPS hospital.

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:18.440
Not just a diagnostic clinic

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:19.320
or a CDT.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:20.120
Okay.

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:20.920
But the day it gets

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:22.760
that IPPS certification,

00:34:22.760 --> 00:34:23.880
the machine turns on.

00:34:24.760 --> 00:34:25.880
Its local payroll

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:26.840
automatically becomes

00:34:26.840 --> 00:34:27.640
the rural floor

00:34:27.640 --> 00:34:28.840
for all of Puerto Rico.

00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:29.480
Automatic.

00:34:29.480 --> 00:34:30.520
Automatically.

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:32.280
There are no petitions to file.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:33.640
No new laws need to be

00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:34.760
debated or passed by

00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:35.480
Congress.

00:34:35.480 --> 00:34:37.640
It is an automatic statutory trigger.

00:34:37.640 --> 00:34:39.160
So the moment they hire staff

00:34:39.160 --> 00:34:40.440
as a certified hospital,

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:42.600
their specific wages

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:43.960
set the basement for every

00:34:43.960 --> 00:34:45.400
single urban hospital

00:34:45.400 --> 00:34:47.320
in San Juan, Ponce,

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:48.680
everywhere else on the main island.

00:34:48.680 --> 00:34:49.160
Yes.

00:34:49.160 --> 00:34:50.440
So what does the math

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:51.240
actually look like?

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:52.520
Like how high does the wage

00:34:52.520 --> 00:34:53.320
NVX need to be

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:54.280
to stop the bleeding?

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:55.400
The target wage isn't just

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:56.520
an aspirational number

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:57.720
picked out of thin air.

00:34:57.720 --> 00:34:59.160
It's pure arithmetic.

00:34:59.160 --> 00:34:59.560
Okay.

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:00.840
Walk me through the arithmetic.

00:35:00.840 --> 00:35:02.280
To calculate the wage index,

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:03.960
you take the average hourly wage

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:05.080
you are paying your staff.

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:07.000
And you divide it by the national

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:08.600
average hourly wage divisor.

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:09.960
And what is that divisor?

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:11.880
For fiscal year 2027,

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.400
CMS has mathematically set

00:35:13.400 --> 00:35:14.680
that national divisor

00:35:14.680 --> 00:35:16.600
at $60.43.

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:17.800
So the formula is

00:35:17.800 --> 00:35:21.000
VX wage divided by $60.43

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:22.920
equals the rural floor index.

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:23.240
Correct.

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:24.440
So let's do an example.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:26.520
If the operators of the VX hospital

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:27.400
fund their payroll

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:28.280
to pay their staff

00:35:28.280 --> 00:35:30.840
an average of $25.40 an hour.

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:31.720
And you divide that

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:34.680
by the $60.43 national divisor.

00:35:34.680 --> 00:35:36.040
You create a wage index floor

00:35:36.040 --> 00:35:37.560
of roughly 0.42.

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:38.760
Okay, 0.42.

00:35:38.760 --> 00:35:40.040
But if they decide to heavily

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:40.920
invest in their staff,

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:41.800
say they pay an average

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:43.960
of $30.25 an hour,

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:44.760
that creates a floor

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:46.280
of roughly 0.50.

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:46.840
And remember,

00:35:46.840 --> 00:35:48.360
the natural unboosted index

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:48.920
for the island

00:35:48.920 --> 00:35:49.960
is currently stepping down

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:51.080
toward 0.35.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:51.480
Right.

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:53.320
So a rural floor of 0.42

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:54.520
or especially 0.50

00:35:54.520 --> 00:35:55.720
would act as this massive

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:57.080
concrete safety net,

00:35:57.080 --> 00:35:58.360
catching every single urban

00:35:58.360 --> 00:35:59.240
hospital in Puerto Rico

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:00.360
before they hit the bottom

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:01.320
exactly the same way

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:02.760
Nantucket caught Boston.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:04.200
It stops the glide path

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:05.800
dead in its tracks

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:07.960
and reverses the trend entirely.

00:36:07.960 --> 00:36:10.440
But there is a massive

00:36:10.440 --> 00:36:12.680
incredibly dangerous trap here.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:14.520
Sequencing is absolutely everything.

00:36:14.520 --> 00:36:15.880
The imputed floor trap.

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:17.080
Walk me through this mechanism

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:18.120
because if the local government

00:36:18.120 --> 00:36:19.640
messes up the sequencing here,

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:21.480
they don't just fail to help.

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:22.760
They actually make the financial

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:24.280
crisis significantly worse.

00:36:24.840 --> 00:36:25.960
Yeah, it could backfire

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:27.160
catastrophically.

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:28.520
Currently, because Puerto Rico

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:30.280
has zero rural hospitals,

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.120
they benefit from a special

00:36:32.120 --> 00:36:33.480
temporary federal provision

00:36:33.480 --> 00:36:35.720
called an imputed statewide floor.

00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:36.840
An imputed floor.

00:36:36.840 --> 00:36:37.800
What does that mean?

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:39.800
It's essentially a placeholder safety net.

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:41.800
For 2027, this imputed floor

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:42.680
provides a safety net

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:44.520
at an index of 0.3278.

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:45.720
OK.

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:46.920
Now it's not a great number,

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:47.880
but it's far better

00:36:47.880 --> 00:36:49.800
than their absolute raw data

00:36:49.800 --> 00:36:50.760
which would drag them down

00:36:50.760 --> 00:36:52.120
to around 0.25.

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:53.480
So they currently have a weak

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:54.840
placeholder safety net

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:56.680
at 0.3278.

00:36:56.680 --> 00:36:57.160
Right.

00:36:57.160 --> 00:36:58.360
But the danger is this.

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:00.600
The law explicitly states

00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:01.640
that the moment a real

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:02.920
rural hospital opens

00:37:02.920 --> 00:37:04.360
and files its first cost report

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:05.320
with CMS,

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:06.760
the imputed floor is legally

00:37:06.760 --> 00:37:07.400
extinguished.

00:37:07.400 --> 00:37:09.000
But the placeholder vanishes.

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:10.600
The real rural data

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:12.440
instantly replaces the placeholder.

00:37:12.440 --> 00:37:13.800
So imagine the Vicks Hospital

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:15.160
opens tomorrow.

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:16.200
But instead of funding

00:37:16.200 --> 00:37:17.080
a high payroll

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:18.520
to create a high floor,

00:37:18.520 --> 00:37:19.640
they just pay standard

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:20.840
depressed Puerto Rican wages

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:22.280
say $15 an hour.

00:37:22.280 --> 00:37:23.160
OK, let's do the math

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:24.360
on $15 an hour.

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:25.560
If you divide $15

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:27.720
by the $60 and 43s divisor,

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:29.160
that translates to a real

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:31.000
rural floor of about 0.25

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:31.800
level one.

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:32.440
Wait.

00:37:32.440 --> 00:37:33.800
So if Vicks opens

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:35.080
paying minimum wage,

00:37:35.080 --> 00:37:36.120
they would accidentally

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:38.520
overwrite the 0.3278

00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:39.560
imputed safety net

00:37:39.560 --> 00:37:40.040
Yes.

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:40.760
And replace it

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:43.240
with a 0.2511 floor.

00:37:43.240 --> 00:37:44.360
They would actively

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:45.160
lower the floor

00:37:45.160 --> 00:37:46.360
for the entire territory

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:48.040
costing the islands hospitals

00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:49.160
millions of dollars.

00:37:49.160 --> 00:37:49.960
Exactly.

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:51.320
A well-meaning rural hospital

00:37:51.320 --> 00:37:52.360
if it's opened carelessly

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:53.400
without properly funded

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:54.440
and planned payrolls

00:37:54.440 --> 00:37:55.720
turns the lever backward.

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:56.600
It becomes a weapon

00:37:56.600 --> 00:37:57.240
against the system

00:37:57.240 --> 00:37:58.040
rather than a shield.

00:37:58.040 --> 00:37:58.600
So they have to get

00:37:58.600 --> 00:37:59.640
the money in place first.

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:01.240
The wage level in Vicks

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:02.360
must be fully funded

00:38:02.360 --> 00:38:04.200
committed to and executing

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:05.080
before it files

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:06.840
its first Medicare cost report.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:08.120
Timing is everything.

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:09.160
Now, some listeners

00:38:09.160 --> 00:38:10.200
who know healthcare finance

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:11.080
might be listening to this

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:11.880
and thinking,

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:13.000
wait, aren't there other ways

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:13.560
to do this?

00:38:14.120 --> 00:38:15.000
What about just taking

00:38:15.000 --> 00:38:16.520
a huge existing hospital

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:17.480
in San Juan

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:18.520
and having them legally

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:20.440
reclassify as rural?

00:38:20.440 --> 00:38:21.400
That is often referred

00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:22.840
to as path B.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:24.120
It's the urban to rural

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:25.240
reclassification

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:27.480
under section 412.103

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:28.840
of the regulations.

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:30.280
And legally,

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:32.040
yes, a massive hospital

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:34.280
with a high payroll in San Juan

00:38:34.280 --> 00:38:36.600
could theoretically petition CMS

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:38.120
to be classified as rural

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:39.480
in order to set the floor.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:40.760
So why not just do that?

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:42.120
Why go through all the trouble

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:44.120
to rely on a brand new

00:38:44.120 --> 00:38:45.160
untested facility

00:38:45.160 --> 00:38:46.200
on a tiny island?

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:47.640
It really comes down

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:49.240
to a classic economic dilemma.

00:38:49.800 --> 00:38:50.840
The free rider problem.

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:51.880
Let's use an analogy.

00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:52.200
Okay.

00:38:52.200 --> 00:38:53.400
Imagine a massive apartment

00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:53.720
building where

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:55.320
the electrical grid is failing.

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:56.200
One tenant decides

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:57.320
to pay out of their own pocket

00:38:57.320 --> 00:38:58.280
to completely upgrade

00:38:58.280 --> 00:38:59.240
the entire building's

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:00.360
electrical system.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:01.560
They bear the massive cost

00:39:01.560 --> 00:39:02.200
themselves.

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:02.600
Right.

00:39:02.600 --> 00:39:04.360
But once the grid is fixed,

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:05.400
all the other landlords

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:06.120
in the building

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:07.080
get to instantly charge

00:39:07.080 --> 00:39:07.720
higher rent

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:09.160
because the building is nicer

00:39:09.160 --> 00:39:10.520
without having spent a dime.

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:11.400
The other landlords

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:12.520
are the free riders.

00:39:12.520 --> 00:39:13.320
Right.

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:14.680
In the hospital scenario,

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:15.800
if a massive hospital

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:17.240
in San Juan decides to

00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:19.400
artificially inflate its wages

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:21.560
to say $30 an hour

00:39:21.560 --> 00:39:22.920
just to set the rural floor,

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:25.800
that specific hospital

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:27.560
has to bear the multimillion

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:28.520
dollar burden

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:30.120
of that inflated payroll

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:31.160
entirely on its own

00:39:31.160 --> 00:39:32.200
balance sheet.

00:39:32.200 --> 00:39:34.200
But the benefit of that action,

00:39:34.200 --> 00:39:35.480
the higher Medicare payments

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:36.760
generated by the new

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:38.600
.50 floor flows

00:39:38.600 --> 00:39:39.720
to every other competing

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:41.000
hospital on the island.

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:42.040
You're basically asking

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:43.160
a hospital CFO

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:44.600
to double their own payroll

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:45.720
costs out of the goodness

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:46.680
of their heart,

00:39:46.680 --> 00:39:47.560
deliberately hurting

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:49.080
their own operating margins

00:39:49.080 --> 00:39:49.880
so that their direct

00:39:49.880 --> 00:39:51.160
competitors across the street

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:52.520
can get a massive revenue

00:39:52.520 --> 00:39:53.400
boost from Medicare.

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:54.040
Exactly.

00:39:54.040 --> 00:39:55.160
And no hospital board

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:56.280
of directors is going to

00:39:56.280 --> 00:39:57.080
sustain that kind

00:39:57.080 --> 00:39:58.520
of corporate altruism.

00:39:58.520 --> 00:39:59.400
They have a fiduciary

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:00.600
duty to their own survival.

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:01.240
Makes sense.

00:40:01.240 --> 00:40:03.400
Nantucket pays $78 an hour

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:04.760
because its own isolated

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:06.360
island economy demands it.

00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:07.320
They aren't doing it to do

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:08.120
Boston a favor.

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:10.040
VAX is the exact same.

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:11.480
It has no free riders.

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:12.600
It is one hospital,

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:13.800
one localized payroll,

00:40:13.800 --> 00:40:15.000
one decision maker.

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:16.040
So we're talking about

00:40:16.040 --> 00:40:17.640
a massive systemic manipulation

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:19.240
based purely on geography.

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:20.600
I have to ask

00:40:20.600 --> 00:40:21.880
the care versus compliance

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:22.600
question here.

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:23.160
Yeah.

00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:24.200
Is this just financial

00:40:24.200 --> 00:40:24.760
engineering?

00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:25.720
Like are we just gaming

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:26.840
a spreadsheet here,

00:40:26.840 --> 00:40:27.960
finding the Puerto Rican

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:29.320
equivalent of the Nantucket

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:30.040
loophole?

00:40:30.040 --> 00:40:31.640
What's fascinating here

00:40:31.640 --> 00:40:33.480
is the moral center

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:35.560
of this specific strategy.

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:36.600
If you reclassify

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:38.680
an urban hospital in San Juan,

00:40:38.680 --> 00:40:40.280
just to manipulate the numbers,

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:41.720
yes, that is pure spreadsheet

00:40:41.720 --> 00:40:42.520
manipulation.

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:42.920
Right.

00:40:42.920 --> 00:40:44.520
But the VAX strategy

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:46.200
is fundamentally different.

00:40:46.200 --> 00:40:47.800
This strategy ends

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:49.880
with a genuinely rural,

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:52.280
geographically isolated island,

00:40:52.280 --> 00:40:53.720
an island where a 13-year-old

00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:55.560
girl died in agonizing death

00:40:55.560 --> 00:40:56.280
because they didn't have a

00:40:56.280 --> 00:40:57.720
basic emergency room,

00:40:57.720 --> 00:40:59.320
finally getting a fully staffed,

00:40:59.320 --> 00:41:01.000
well-paid, highly competent

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:01.880
medical team.

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:02.920
The policy lever

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:03.720
and the human need

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:05.560
are the exact same object.

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:06.760
By funding the doctors,

00:41:06.760 --> 00:41:07.880
nurses, and technicians

00:41:07.880 --> 00:41:09.000
in VAX at a high level

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:10.360
to save lives locally,

00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:11.640
you automatically trigger

00:41:11.640 --> 00:41:12.600
the federal formula

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:13.480
that saves the healthcare

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:14.760
system territorially.

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:16.520
It aligns regulatory compliance

00:41:16.520 --> 00:41:18.440
perfectly with urgent patient care.

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:20.360
It is using the system's

00:41:20.360 --> 00:41:21.800
own rules to correct

00:41:21.800 --> 00:41:23.880
a massive historical inequity.

00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:25.800
So the strategy is perfectly legal,

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:27.400
the math is totally sound,

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:28.600
and the moral imperative

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:29.800
is extremely clear.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:30.360
Right.

00:41:30.360 --> 00:41:31.240
But we need to look

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:32.360
at where things stand

00:41:32.360 --> 00:41:33.800
right now in the real world.

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:35.640
Let's fast forward

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:36.760
to our current status.

00:41:37.480 --> 00:41:39.800
It is September 1st, 2026.

00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:41.880
The building is physically

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:43.160
sitting there on the island.

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:45.080
What is the status

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:47.560
of this $85 million facility?

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:48.760
Well, the physical infrastructure

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:50.280
is incredibly impressive.

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:51.320
The building was officially

00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:52.280
delivered to the Puerto Rico

00:41:52.280 --> 00:41:53.080
Department of Health

00:41:53.080 --> 00:41:54.520
in December of 2025.

00:41:54.520 --> 00:41:55.640
And who paid for it?

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:57.800
It's an $85 million investment

00:41:57.880 --> 00:41:59.560
funded largely by FEMA,

00:41:59.560 --> 00:42:01.320
along with local government funds.

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:02.040
And they didn't just

00:42:02.040 --> 00:42:03.400
build a standard clinic,

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:05.640
they built it for deep resilience.

00:42:05.640 --> 00:42:07.000
It is green certified

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:09.320
under very strict resilience principles.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:10.920
Yeah, I read the specs on this place

00:42:10.920 --> 00:42:12.680
and it is basically a fortress.

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:15.720
It has 21 days of resilience autonomy.

00:42:15.720 --> 00:42:17.000
Which means it can run on its own

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:17.720
for three weeks?

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:18.280
Exactly.

00:42:18.280 --> 00:42:19.640
It has two massive

00:42:19.640 --> 00:42:20.760
redundant generators.

00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:21.880
It has redundant chillers

00:42:21.880 --> 00:42:23.320
for the air conditioning.

00:42:23.320 --> 00:42:24.760
And water tower is holding

00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:25.960
70,000 gallons

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:27.160
of potable water.

00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:29.400
It is explicitly built to survive

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:31.000
the next hurricane Maria

00:42:31.000 --> 00:42:32.760
without losing power or water

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:33.800
for three weeks.

00:42:33.800 --> 00:42:34.600
And the helipad,

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:36.200
which is crucial for those

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:37.160
emergency transfers

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:38.360
that failed JADALYZ

00:42:38.360 --> 00:42:39.880
back in 2020,

00:42:39.880 --> 00:42:41.880
is fully FAA accredited

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:43.880
as of April 2026.

00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:44.600
Yes.

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:46.040
And we're already seeing

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:47.400
successful private public

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:49.240
collaboration on the ground there

00:42:49.240 --> 00:42:50.440
proving that the logistics

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:51.560
of operating on VAICS

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:52.680
can actually work.

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:53.800
Give me an example.

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:54.920
The Dialysis Center

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:55.960
inside the facility,

00:42:55.960 --> 00:42:57.880
which is run by a private operator

00:42:57.880 --> 00:42:59.560
for Cineus Medical Care,

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:01.000
has been successfully operating

00:43:01.000 --> 00:43:01.880
and treating patients

00:43:01.880 --> 00:43:03.320
since October 2025.

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:04.120
Oh, that's great.

00:43:04.120 --> 00:43:05.320
Furthermore, the construction

00:43:05.320 --> 00:43:06.680
itself was brought to the finish

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:07.240
line

00:43:07.240 --> 00:43:08.680
by a private contractor,

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:09.640
Korea Bay Techno.

00:43:09.640 --> 00:43:10.920
So the private sector is proving

00:43:10.920 --> 00:43:12.520
they can execute on VAICS,

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:14.280
but what about the main emergency

00:43:14.280 --> 00:43:16.120
room, the core of the hospital

00:43:16.120 --> 00:43:17.560
that actually sets this whole

00:43:17.560 --> 00:43:18.680
wage index lever?

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:19.160
Right.

00:43:19.160 --> 00:43:20.680
It's still not fully operational.

00:43:20.680 --> 00:43:21.240
Why not?

00:43:21.240 --> 00:43:22.680
The contract for the primary

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:23.640
private operator,

00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:24.920
the CDT,

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:27.960
is currently stalled in negotiations.

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:29.400
The government issued a request

00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:30.520
for proposals,

00:43:30.520 --> 00:43:32.040
but they only received one bid.

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:33.640
So as of right now,

00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:34.520
the Department of Health's

00:43:34.520 --> 00:43:35.560
Field Medicine Unit

00:43:35.560 --> 00:43:37.400
is just covering emergency care

00:43:37.400 --> 00:43:38.600
on a temporary basis.

00:43:38.600 --> 00:43:40.040
And here is the single most

00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:41.560
critical bureaucratic hurdle

00:43:41.560 --> 00:43:42.920
for the local government.

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:44.600
The Puerto Rico Department of Health

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:46.520
must make one crucial

00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:47.880
administrative move.

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:49.320
Yes, this is everything.

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:51.080
They absolutely have to license

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:51.960
this new facility

00:43:51.960 --> 00:43:54.120
as a hospital with inpatient beds

00:43:54.120 --> 00:43:55.400
if they simply license it

00:43:55.400 --> 00:43:57.240
as a diagnostic and treatment center,

00:43:57.240 --> 00:43:58.040
a CDT,

00:43:58.040 --> 00:44:00.280
exactly like the old facility was,

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:01.000
it doesn't count.

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:01.160
Right.

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:02.600
Because CDTs don't file

00:44:02.600 --> 00:44:03.960
IPPS cost reports.

00:44:04.520 --> 00:44:06.200
The wage data goes nowhere

00:44:06.200 --> 00:44:07.400
and the systemic lever

00:44:07.400 --> 00:44:08.600
is completely useless.

00:44:08.600 --> 00:44:10.520
The entire territorial strategy

00:44:10.520 --> 00:44:11.720
hinges on that single

00:44:11.720 --> 00:44:12.840
licensure decision.

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:14.120
It must be legally classified

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:14.760
as a hospital.

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:15.880
And because of that,

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:16.920
the political pressure

00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:18.360
is mounting intensely right now.

00:44:18.920 --> 00:44:19.800
Just recently,

00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:21.800
on August 28, 2026,

00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:23.080
there was a major surprise

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:24.360
visit to the facility

00:44:24.360 --> 00:44:25.480
by high-ranking official.

00:44:25.480 --> 00:44:26.360
Who is there?

00:44:26.360 --> 00:44:28.760
Senate President Thomas Rivera Schatz,

00:44:28.760 --> 00:44:31.000
former Governor Pedro Pierluisi,

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:32.360
and the current Health Secretary,

00:44:32.360 --> 00:44:33.400
Victor Ramos,

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:34.680
all converged on the site

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:35.880
to inspect the progress.

00:44:35.880 --> 00:44:37.400
And what came out of that visit?

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:39.560
Well, to report impartially on it,

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:41.480
Secretary Ramos strongly defended

00:44:41.480 --> 00:44:43.240
the administration's progress.

00:44:43.240 --> 00:44:45.160
He highlighted a significant victory.

00:44:45.880 --> 00:44:48.360
A new modern CT scanner

00:44:48.360 --> 00:44:49.640
had just arrived on the island

00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:50.440
the day before.

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:51.400
That's a big deal.

00:44:51.400 --> 00:44:52.440
It was a critical point

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:53.720
because it rectified

00:44:53.720 --> 00:44:55.880
a highly criticized past issue

00:44:55.880 --> 00:44:57.400
where a mobile CT unit

00:44:57.400 --> 00:44:58.840
was purchased for the island.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:44:59.800
But it was just left sitting

00:44:59.800 --> 00:45:01.560
in a warehouse on the main island

00:45:01.560 --> 00:45:03.640
until this warranty completely expired.

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:04.600
Oh, man.

00:45:04.600 --> 00:45:05.320
Yeah.

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:06.920
But Secretary Ramos maintained

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:07.880
during the visit

00:45:07.880 --> 00:45:08.760
that they are targeting

00:45:08.760 --> 00:45:10.360
a late September inauguration

00:45:10.360 --> 00:45:12.440
pending final equipment deliveries,

00:45:12.440 --> 00:45:13.160
health inspections,

00:45:13.160 --> 00:45:14.120
and crucially,

00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:16.280
that complex licensure process.

00:45:16.280 --> 00:45:17.720
It's a perfect analogy.

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:19.000
The facility right now

00:45:19.000 --> 00:45:20.200
is like a brand new

00:45:20.200 --> 00:45:21.400
fully-fueled jet

00:45:21.400 --> 00:45:22.600
sitting on the runway.

00:45:22.600 --> 00:45:23.720
The engines are humming.

00:45:23.720 --> 00:45:24.840
The private sector pilots

00:45:24.840 --> 00:45:25.800
are ready to get in the seats.

00:45:25.800 --> 00:45:27.320
The passengers are waiting,

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:28.840
but the plane cannot take off

00:45:28.840 --> 00:45:29.880
because they are just waiting

00:45:29.880 --> 00:45:30.920
for the control tower

00:45:30.920 --> 00:45:32.360
to hand over the final

00:45:32.360 --> 00:45:34.040
stamp registration papers.

00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:35.560
That's exactly where they are.

00:45:35.560 --> 00:45:37.480
The high-profile political visit

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:40.120
just underscores the intense oversight

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:42.360
required to push the bureaucracy forward.

00:45:43.240 --> 00:45:44.760
Every single day,

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:46.520
this building sits classified

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:49.160
as a CDT or remains unopened.

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:51.000
The island misses the opportunity

00:45:51.080 --> 00:45:52.200
to start the clock

00:45:52.200 --> 00:45:53.720
on this multi-million dollar

00:45:53.720 --> 00:45:54.760
financial lever.

00:45:54.760 --> 00:45:56.280
Which brings us to the final

00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:57.720
and perhaps most daunting

00:45:57.720 --> 00:45:58.760
logistical challenge

00:45:58.760 --> 00:45:59.960
of this entire endeavor.

00:46:00.680 --> 00:46:01.880
The timeline lag.

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:03.160
The time delay, yes.

00:46:03.160 --> 00:46:04.840
Because even if the Department of Health

00:46:04.840 --> 00:46:06.040
signs the hospital license

00:46:06.040 --> 00:46:07.000
tomorrow morning

00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:08.760
and the facility opens fully staffed

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:10.360
paying $30 an hour,

00:46:10.360 --> 00:46:11.240
the spreadsheet

00:46:11.240 --> 00:46:12.840
does not update overnight.

00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:14.280
Right, because federal bureaucracy

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:15.480
is never instantaneous.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:17.400
There is a built-in time delay.

00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:18.600
Walk us through the clock on this.

00:46:18.600 --> 00:46:20.360
Why doesn't the money flow immediately?

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:22.760
Because Medicare builds its wage index

00:46:22.760 --> 00:46:24.680
using audited cost reports.

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:26.680
And those reports are, by definition,

00:46:26.680 --> 00:46:27.480
historical.

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:28.200
Right.

00:46:28.200 --> 00:46:29.320
It takes time for a hospital

00:46:29.320 --> 00:46:30.840
to finish a fiscal year,

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:32.360
compile their payroll data,

00:46:32.360 --> 00:46:33.880
file the complex report,

00:46:33.880 --> 00:46:35.880
have an independent auditor review it,

00:46:35.880 --> 00:46:37.800
and then have CMS actually integrate

00:46:37.800 --> 00:46:38.760
that data

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:40.120
into the massive national

00:46:40.120 --> 00:46:41.240
rule-making cycle.

00:46:42.280 --> 00:46:43.080
Generally speaking,

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:44.760
the wage index is based on data

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:46.920
that is roughly four years old.

00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:47.720
Four years.

00:46:47.720 --> 00:46:49.080
Okay, let's play this timeline out

00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:51.400
so we really understand the gap here.

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:53.320
If the VX hospital opens

00:46:53.320 --> 00:46:55.400
and is certified as an IPPS hospital

00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:57.400
in fiscal year 2027.

00:46:57.400 --> 00:46:59.400
It's high wages wouldn't actually hit

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:01.400
the published operational wage index

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:03.400
until fiscal year 2031.

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:04.760
So the San Juan hospitals wouldn't

00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:06.920
see the benefit until 2031.

00:47:06.920 --> 00:47:07.560
Yes.

00:47:07.560 --> 00:47:08.120
And remember,

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:09.240
the repricing mechanism

00:47:09.240 --> 00:47:10.040
for Medicare Advantage

00:47:10.040 --> 00:47:10.920
we discussed earlier.

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:11.640
Oh, right.

00:47:11.640 --> 00:47:12.120
The AGAs.

00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:13.800
Do you know how the AGAs calculated?

00:47:13.800 --> 00:47:16.440
That adds roughly another calendar year of delay.

00:47:16.440 --> 00:47:18.040
So those higher wages from VX

00:47:18.040 --> 00:47:19.800
wouldn't fully hit and stabilize

00:47:19.800 --> 00:47:21.560
the Medicare Advantage benchmarks

00:47:21.560 --> 00:47:23.560
until calendar year 2032.

00:47:23.560 --> 00:47:25.880
Okay, 2032 is the finish line,

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:28.280
but the 5% cuts from the bridgeport

00:47:28.280 --> 00:47:29.960
ruling are happening right now.

00:47:29.960 --> 00:47:30.680
Exactly.

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:34.920
The 5% cuts for 2025, 2026, and 2027

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:36.920
are already cascading through the system,

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:38.040
cutting revenue today.

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:41.320
The calendar year 2028 Medicare Advantage

00:47:41.320 --> 00:47:42.360
benchmarks,

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:44.280
which are currently being formulated,

00:47:44.280 --> 00:47:46.840
already have those compounding cuts locked in.

00:47:46.840 --> 00:47:47.640
Wow.

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:49.960
So there is a definitive four-year gap

00:47:49.960 --> 00:47:52.600
from roughly 2028 to 2032

00:47:53.160 --> 00:47:55.240
where the VX data hasn't ripened yet,

00:47:55.240 --> 00:47:56.280
but the bridgeport cuts

00:47:56.280 --> 00:47:58.200
are fully active and accelerating.

00:47:58.200 --> 00:47:59.400
The island's healthcare system

00:47:59.400 --> 00:48:01.000
will bleed millions of dollars

00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:02.520
significantly during that window.

00:48:02.520 --> 00:48:04.120
So how do they survive the gap?

00:48:04.120 --> 00:48:05.400
You can't just tell an ICU

00:48:05.400 --> 00:48:07.000
to hold its breath for four years

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:09.000
while waiting for a spreadsheet to update.

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:10.440
This raises an important question

00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:12.600
about how you enact durable systemic change.

00:48:12.600 --> 00:48:13.560
You cannot just rely

00:48:13.560 --> 00:48:15.640
on the geographic solution on the ground.

00:48:15.640 --> 00:48:17.080
You have to run a parallel track

00:48:17.160 --> 00:48:19.640
of intense administrative advocacy in Washington.

00:48:19.640 --> 00:48:20.680
The island needs to show up

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:21.560
in the federal docket

00:48:21.560 --> 00:48:22.680
and fight for a bridge.

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:23.480
They do.

00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:24.760
And the docket participation

00:48:24.760 --> 00:48:27.000
is a shockingly weak point right now.

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:27.960
This is a statistic

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:29.480
that absolutely floored me

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:31.000
when we were researching this.

00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:34.280
When CMS published the fiscal year 2027

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:36.200
IPPS proposed rule,

00:48:36.200 --> 00:48:37.320
the specific rule

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:39.320
that executed the third consecutive

00:48:39.320 --> 00:48:41.960
5% cut to Puerto Rico's hospitals,

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:44.520
there were 982 public comments

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:46.200
filed nationally by hospitals,

00:48:46.200 --> 00:48:47.800
associations and advocates.

00:48:47.800 --> 00:48:50.280
982 comments across the country.

00:48:50.280 --> 00:48:52.760
Do you know how many of those 982 comments

00:48:52.760 --> 00:48:54.840
came from institutions in Puerto Rico

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:55.800
fighting the cut?

00:48:55.800 --> 00:48:56.360
Four.

00:48:56.360 --> 00:48:58.360
Four out of nearly a thousand.

00:48:58.360 --> 00:48:59.640
Four comments representing

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:00.760
an entire territory

00:49:00.760 --> 00:49:02.040
facing a financial crisis.

00:49:02.040 --> 00:49:02.840
It's unbelievable.

00:49:02.840 --> 00:49:05.240
You simply cannot win a bureaucratic war

00:49:05.240 --> 00:49:06.200
if you do not show up

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:07.880
on the administrative battlefield.

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:09.560
The hospitals, the associations,

00:49:09.560 --> 00:49:10.280
the public,

00:49:10.280 --> 00:49:12.040
they absolutely must mobilize

00:49:12.040 --> 00:49:13.320
to comment on the upcoming

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:15.560
FY 2028 proposed rule.

00:49:15.640 --> 00:49:17.480
Explicitly asking CMS to use

00:49:17.480 --> 00:49:18.760
its administrative discretion

00:49:18.760 --> 00:49:20.600
to hold or pause the transition cuts

00:49:20.600 --> 00:49:22.680
while the new VX wage data matures.

00:49:22.680 --> 00:49:24.120
And there is a secondary,

00:49:24.120 --> 00:49:25.160
highly specific,

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:27.240
statutory channel available as well.

00:49:28.200 --> 00:49:30.040
Under section 1853

00:49:30.040 --> 00:49:31.640
of the Social Security Act,

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:33.000
the governor of a territory

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:34.920
has a formal legal mechanism

00:49:34.920 --> 00:49:36.120
to request adjustments

00:49:36.120 --> 00:49:38.360
to the Medicare Advantage payment methodology

00:49:38.360 --> 00:49:39.480
directly from the Secretary

00:49:39.480 --> 00:49:40.920
of Health and Human Services.

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:42.600
So the governor can bypass

00:49:42.600 --> 00:49:44.120
the standard public comment section

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:45.720
and go straight to the top of the agency.

00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:47.960
Yes, but there are strict,

00:49:47.960 --> 00:49:49.160
unforgiving deadlines.

00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:52.680
To affect the crucial 2028 benchmarks,

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:53.800
the governor must submit

00:49:53.800 --> 00:49:55.480
this formal data-driven request

00:49:55.480 --> 00:49:57.720
by February 1st, 2027.

00:49:57.720 --> 00:49:59.640
What exactly should they be asking for?

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:01.480
The specific ask being championed

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:02.840
by several congressional leaders

00:50:02.840 --> 00:50:04.040
and policy experts

00:50:04.040 --> 00:50:05.320
is for the governor to request

00:50:05.320 --> 00:50:06.200
a stabilization of

00:50:06.200 --> 00:50:08.280
Puerto Rico's average geographic adjustment,

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:09.160
the AGA,

00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:11.240
at a minimum floor of 0.7 year.

00:50:11.240 --> 00:50:13.400
And that 0.70 AGA

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:15.240
acts as the bridge over the gap.

00:50:15.240 --> 00:50:15.960
Exactly.

00:50:15.960 --> 00:50:16.920
It freezes the cuts

00:50:16.920 --> 00:50:18.120
and stabilizes the funding

00:50:18.120 --> 00:50:21.400
across that brutal 2028-2032 gap.

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:22.520
It stops the slow bleed

00:50:22.520 --> 00:50:23.160
just long enough

00:50:23.160 --> 00:50:24.360
for the real hard data

00:50:24.360 --> 00:50:25.400
from the Vieques hospital

00:50:25.400 --> 00:50:26.680
to finally enter the spreadsheet,

00:50:26.680 --> 00:50:27.880
percolate through the audits,

00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:29.160
and take over permanently.

00:50:29.160 --> 00:50:30.520
So you fight on two fronts.

00:50:30.520 --> 00:50:32.120
You set up the geographic solution

00:50:32.120 --> 00:50:33.720
on the ground in Vieques

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:35.160
while simultaneously fighting

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:37.240
the administrative battle in Washington

00:50:37.240 --> 00:50:38.360
to buy the time you need

00:50:38.360 --> 00:50:39.720
for the solution to work.

00:50:39.720 --> 00:50:40.200
Wow.

00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:41.320
Okay, let's bring this all back

00:50:41.320 --> 00:50:42.520
to where we started today.

00:50:42.600 --> 00:50:44.600
The core paradox

00:50:44.600 --> 00:50:45.720
of these two islands.

00:50:45.720 --> 00:50:47.000
The tale of two islands.

00:50:47.000 --> 00:50:48.040
We have this finished

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:50.600
$85 million hurricane-proof building

00:50:50.600 --> 00:50:51.320
sitting right now

00:50:51.320 --> 00:50:52.360
on the island of Vieques.

00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:54.840
And it is two profound things

00:50:54.840 --> 00:50:56.360
at the exact same time.

00:50:56.360 --> 00:50:57.480
First and foremost,

00:50:57.480 --> 00:50:58.600
it is a life-saving

00:50:58.600 --> 00:51:00.840
emergency room and maternity ward

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:03.160
for 8,000 American citizens

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:04.120
who have endured

00:51:04.120 --> 00:51:05.720
the unimaginable trauma

00:51:05.720 --> 00:51:06.840
of lacking basic care.

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:09.000
A place where a 13-year-old girl

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:09.880
shouldn't have to die

00:51:09.880 --> 00:51:11.160
waiting for a ventilator.

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:11.720
Yes.

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:13.880
But second, it is a massive

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:16.120
multimillion-dollar financial lever

00:51:16.120 --> 00:51:17.480
for an entire territory

00:51:17.480 --> 00:51:19.480
of 3.2 million people.

00:51:19.480 --> 00:51:21.080
It proves that the vast difference

00:51:21.080 --> 00:51:22.200
between the exorbitant wealth

00:51:22.200 --> 00:51:23.720
of Nantucket's hospitals

00:51:23.720 --> 00:51:24.920
and the compounding poverty

00:51:24.920 --> 00:51:26.520
of Puerto Rico's hospitals

00:51:26.520 --> 00:51:28.040
wasn't ever really about

00:51:28.040 --> 00:51:29.000
the physical ocean

00:51:29.000 --> 00:51:29.640
separating them

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:30.600
from the mainland.

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:31.640
It was purely about

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:33.480
who had the foresight

00:51:33.480 --> 00:51:34.840
or the historical luck

00:51:34.840 --> 00:51:36.040
to place a certified

00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:37.400
IPPS hospital

00:51:37.400 --> 00:51:38.920
on the geographic playing field

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:40.280
and who knew how to master

00:51:40.280 --> 00:51:41.320
the rules of the game.

00:51:41.320 --> 00:51:42.440
It's all about knowing

00:51:42.440 --> 00:51:43.640
how the board is set up.

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:45.320
I want to leave you, the listener,

00:51:45.320 --> 00:51:46.680
with something to mull over today

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:48.440
as we wrap up this deep dive.

00:51:48.440 --> 00:51:49.640
A final thought.

00:51:49.640 --> 00:51:51.880
Think about your own local hospital.

00:51:51.880 --> 00:51:52.760
Think about the care

00:51:52.760 --> 00:51:53.960
your community receives

00:51:53.960 --> 00:51:55.320
the nurses at the bedside,

00:51:55.320 --> 00:51:56.520
the equipment and the ER.

00:51:57.160 --> 00:51:58.760
How much of modern healthcare

00:51:58.760 --> 00:51:59.480
is dictated

00:51:59.480 --> 00:52:01.160
not by actual medical need

00:52:01.160 --> 00:52:02.920
or the actual cost of a syringe

00:52:02.920 --> 00:52:04.680
or the skill of a local surgeon,

00:52:05.400 --> 00:52:06.920
but by the legal definition

00:52:06.920 --> 00:52:07.960
of a zip code?

00:52:07.960 --> 00:52:09.240
It's a sobering thought.

00:52:09.240 --> 00:52:10.200
When a health system's

00:52:10.200 --> 00:52:11.000
very survival

00:52:11.000 --> 00:52:12.120
depends entirely on

00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:14.120
mastering geographic legal fictions,

00:52:14.760 --> 00:52:16.600
are we optimizing for patient care

00:52:16.600 --> 00:52:18.600
or are we just optimizing for compliance?

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:20.360
That's the million-dollar question.

00:52:20.360 --> 00:52:21.720
So what does this all mean for you?

00:52:22.280 --> 00:52:24.680
Next time you drive past a rural clinic

00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:26.920
or a massive urban medical center,

00:52:26.920 --> 00:52:28.840
remember that there are invisible lines

00:52:28.840 --> 00:52:30.040
and federal spreadsheets

00:52:30.040 --> 00:52:32.360
dictating what happens inside those walls

00:52:32.360 --> 00:52:34.200
long before you ever walk through the doors.

00:52:34.760 --> 00:52:36.280
The machinery is complex,

00:52:36.280 --> 00:52:38.280
abstract, and deeply bureaucratic,

00:52:38.280 --> 00:52:39.720
but as we saw in VX,

00:52:39.800 --> 00:52:41.640
the outcomes are intensely,

00:52:41.640 --> 00:52:43.320
sometimes tragically human.

00:52:43.320 --> 00:52:44.040
Yes, sir.

00:52:44.040 --> 00:52:45.480
Thanks for joining us for this deep dive.

00:52:45.480 --> 00:52:46.440
We'll see you next time.

